<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Niger</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Niamey</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked Sahel state, independent from France since 1960, whose history since has included five coups and four periods of military rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-niger">Colony of Niger</a>, a distinct colony within French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> since 1922, became an autonomous republic on 18 December 1958 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamani-diori">Hamani Diori</a>. It left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> and became fully independent on 3 August 1960, with Diori as its first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Diori ruled a single-party civilian state for 14 years, allowing French-led uranium mining at Arlit, until a coup in 1974 brought Colonel Seyni Kountché to power. Kountché ruled until his death in 1987 and was succeeded by Ali Saibou, who began political reform. The National Sovereign Conference of 1991 introduced multi-party democracy, and the Third Republic followed. Coups in 1996 and 2010 interrupted civilian government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> rebellions broke out in the north in the 1990s and again in 2007. A constitution of 2010 set up a multiparty, semi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presidential-democracy">presidential system</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Mahamadou Issoufou was president from 2011, and Mohamed Bazoum succeeded him after elections in December 2020 and a run-off. On 26 July 2023 the military overthrew Bazoum, and General Abdourahamane Tiani was proclaimed head of state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> replaced French as the official language under a transitional charter in 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/niger.jpg" alt="Niger" width="330" height="283"><figcaption>Image: Made by: Philippe Verdy verdy_p, see also fr:Utilisateur:verdy_p. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Niger.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Niamey</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hamani Diori, Seyni Kountché, Ali Saibou, Mahamane Ousmane, Mamadou Tandja</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (99.3% in the 2012 census), Christianity, traditional beliefs</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa, French, Zarma and Songhay, Tamasheq, Kanuri, Fula</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/niger">Niger</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of the Niger</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=niger" aria-label="Niger on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Niger lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5176 3855L5176 3855"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5176 3855h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5176 3855h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5176 3855h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Niamey. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=niger">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
